(*I'm making some progress in creating a prototype for a submission system=
=20
(in order to show you all, test, validate, etc.). But I have a few pending=
=20
issues. I'll let you know!*)
Meanwhile...

*Protection against bashing and other biases*
I had a discussion with someone and he told me the current system design=20
may be subject to a (small) risk of "user bashing". If someone doesn't=20
please people (for instance by saying things unrelated to Lojban, about=20
religion, politics, etc.) he/she may be unfairly "punished" by other=20
people, lowering their grade towards this user. As a result he/she may=20
quickly loose permissions even though the contribution from that user was=
=20
important and his/her Lojban level is very high.
Some other biases of the same kind may exist, such as the opposite behavior=
=20
(a numerous group of people with no knowledge, getting over the community=
=20
with unfair voting), similar to the "51% attack".

Actually the risk is supposedly very low at the beginning (initial people=
=20
are generally fair about each other, because very involved). Then the risk=
=20
grows as newcomers arrive, but decreases with the number of users (the more=
=20
users, the smaller impact each will have). So this is quite important to=20
address this issue now, in my opinion.

However, I'm convinced there is no way to predict all "unfair" behaviors,=
=20
and that even powerful and costly algorithms can't help us much. This is=20
why I'm relaying the solution which was proposed to me (with minor=20
adaptations):

   - Let N people be "guardians":
      - They would have the role of fighting unfair behaviors (and only=20
      this role).
      - And they would have administrative privileges (over the system) for=
=20
      this: ban people, restore some permissions, etc.
      - Hopefully, they would almost never have to do so...
   - Make sure all their actions (as "guardians") are logged and=20
   documented, and publicly accessible. Everyone could then ensure their ro=
le=20
   is respected (=3D that they do not use their permissions to tackle other=
=20
   topics, such as Lojban-related ones).
   - One user may have various roles AND a "guardian" role at the same=20
   time, of course.
   - Maybe that would require two distinct user accounts?
   - Elect those "guardians" periodically (say each 6 months? 1 year?).=20
   Election would use common rules:
   - Everyone can be candidate.
         - If there are less than N candidates, then missing candidates are=
=20
         algorithmically found (Maybe the ones with most permissions? Or si=
mply=20
         randomly?).
      - Each user can vote.
         - Can he/she can vote for N candidates? Or maybe just one?
      - The N users with most votes "win".
  =20
What do you think about it?

la .sykyndyr.



